<section class="content">

<h2>Notes for the Catechist</h2>

<p><strong>1. Sensitivity is essential.</strong> Many people carry deep wounds related to sin and forgiveness. Some have experienced harsh or shaming confessions in the past. Create an atmosphere of mercy, not judgment. The goal is to help students desire the sacrament, not fear it.</p>

<p><strong>2. The seal of confession can be a powerful teaching point.</strong> The fact that a priest would die rather than reveal a confession — and many have — speaks to the absolute sacredness of this sacrament. Students are often amazed by this.</p>

<p><strong>3. Address the "why a priest?" objection head-on.</strong> The most common objection to confession is "Why can't I just confess directly to God?" The answer is that you can and should — but Christ chose to give his Church a visible, sacramental means of forgiveness. If God became man in Jesus, why wouldn't he also use human instruments to convey his mercy?</p>

<p><strong>4. Emphasize frequency.</strong> The Church requires confession at least once a year for grave sins, but recommends frequent confession even for venial sins. Monthly confession is a healthy spiritual rhythm. It's like a shower for the soul — you don't wait until you're filthy to bathe.</p>

<p><strong>5. Practical preparation.</strong> If possible, give students a printed examination of conscience guide. Walk them through how to prepare before coming to the sacrament. Many people avoid confession simply because they don't know how to prepare.</p>

<p><strong>6. Distinguish guilt from shame.</strong> Guilt says "I did something bad"; shame says "I am bad." The sacrament heals guilt and destroys shame. Make this distinction clearly — it is liberating for many.</p>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h2>Adaptation by Age</h2>

<div class="depth-box depth-box--semilla">
<h4 class="depth-box__title">🌱 Ages 6-8 &mdash; Seed</h4>
<p>Focus on the story of the prodigal son. Use simple language: "When we do something wrong, we can say sorry to Jesus through the priest, and Jesus always forgives us." Teach the Act of Contrition as a bedtime prayer. Emphasize that confession is a happy moment — like the party the father threw for his son.</p>
</div>

<div class="depth-box depth-box--brotes">
<h4 class="depth-box__title">🌿 Ages 9-11 &mdash; Sprouts</h4>
<p>Introduce the four steps of confession. Practice with a "mock confession" (not real sins!) so they know the mechanics: how to enter, what to say, when to make the sign of the cross. Explain the seal of confession. Tell the story of St. John Nepomucene.</p>
</div>

<div class="depth-box depth-box--raiz">
<h4 class="depth-box__title">🪴 Ages 12-14 &mdash; Root</h4>
<p>Dive deeper into contrition (perfect vs. imperfect) and why confession of mortal sins in species and number matters. Discuss the three conditions for mortal sin. Address the "why a priest?" question with theological depth. Introduce indulgences and the communion of saints.</p>
</div>

<div class="depth-box depth-box--arbol">
<h4 class="depth-box__title">🌳 Ages 15+ &mdash; Tree</h4>
<p>Full theological exploration. Discuss the history of the sacrament (early Church public penance, Irish monastic influence, Council of Trent). Connect to the Paschal Mystery — every confession participates in Christ's death and resurrection. Discuss the relationship between Confession, Eucharist, and the mission of the Church. Explore contemporary challenges: habitual sin, addiction, the role of counseling alongside confession.</p>
</div>

</section>

