<section class="content">

<h2>Difficult Questions</h2>

<p><strong>Q: "Can a priest tell the police what I confessed?"</strong><br/>
A: Never. Under no circumstances. The seal of confession is absolute. A priest cannot reveal anything he hears in confession even under threat of imprisonment or death. In most countries, the seal of confession is also protected by law.</p>

<p><strong>Q: "What if the priest judges me or thinks badly of me?"</strong><br/>
A: Priests have heard thousands of confessions. They are trained to be instruments of mercy, not judges. Most priests say they are inspired, not scandalized, by the confessions they hear. And every priest knows he is also a sinner who goes to confession himself.</p>

<p><strong>Q: "I keep confessing the same sins over and over. Does that mean God isn't forgiving me?"</strong><br/>
A: Not at all. God's forgiveness is infinite. The fact that you struggle with the same sins means you are in a spiritual battle — and you keep bringing those sins to the sacrament, which is exactly the right thing to do. With each confession, grace strengthens you. Slow growth is still growth.</p>

<p><strong>Q: "What if I'm not sorry enough?"</strong><br/>
A: The very fact that you are in the confessional, that you have made the effort to come, is a sign of contrition. Imperfect contrition (attrition) — sorrow out of fear of hell or the ugliness of sin — is sufficient for the sacrament. The sacrament itself deepens our contrition. Don't let the perfect be the enemy of the good.</p>

<p><strong>Q: "What's the difference between a sin and a psychological problem?"</strong><br/>
A: This is important. Psychological conditions (depression, addiction, OCD) can reduce culpability (CCC 1735). A person with clinical depression might not be able to "choose joy" by will alone. The Church recognizes that mental health and sin are distinct, though they can interact. Sometimes confession needs to be accompanied by professional counseling — and that's perfectly okay.</p>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h2>Catechist's Prayer</h2>

<p>
Lord Jesus, gentle physician of souls, thank you for entrusting me with the ministry of teaching others about your mercy. Give me the wisdom to speak of this sacrament with tenderness and truth. May I never make anyone feel judged or condemned, but always welcomed and loved. Help me be, like the father of the prodigal son, someone who runs toward others with open arms.</p>

<p>St. John Vianney, patron of confessors, pray for us.<br/>
St. John Nepomucene, martyr of the seal, pray for us.<br/>
St. Padre Pio, faithful confessor, pray for us.</p>

<p><em>Amen.</em></p>

